A||N||14||BlackARROW||Clutch? Fork? How the hell?||||11||
Z||000000||BlackARROW||06-01-2001||10:51 PM||||Hi to every Fiero Folks.<P>Well here's another story.<P>I've been driving casually yesterday, having some fun in the sun; the typical Fiero ride I guess. For some times, I had hard time shifting, but I'm aware I have the aluminium pedals.. so no big deal. Yesterday I planned some minor fixes on my car.  One thing I had a hardtime (didn't suceed yet) is to remove the Thermostat cap  (you know, push and clockwise some bit - well it doesn't push very far.  Any tricks?)<P>My big thing is this, I wanted to lubricate my transmission section, as I do some times to help my shifting issue.  To see how this whole thing works, since I was alone, I wanted to simulate the clutch process, than a gear shifting, from the rear.  Instead of pulling out the rod from the slave cylinder, I pushed it in.  It came back, but I think this is where everything started.<P>I then did some things at the pedal, put some lube and the spring and in that plastic thing (with a "spring" in it - I guess.  What's the purpose of that thing anyway?)  Then I tried to start it to see if it was better.  Surprise:  No more shift possible, like the clutch is not releasing. Only in reverse I could get something:  a grind with a slight backward motion.  Woo  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>I tried to bleed the clutch.  Actually I've done it some times.  Now I know how to proceed.  I used Archie's method (also, I bled the slave cylinder).  Since I lube some spring near the clutch pedal, I feel it less hard to action, but then it doesn't do any action still.  <P>I don't know what to think. What could I have done wrong?  According to me, pushing that rod into the slave cylinder should have no impact what so ever to what I have now?!  I have checked for sign of faulty from both the master and the slave cylinder, and they look ok.  Now when the clutch is pressed, the rod is pushed from the slave to I don't know..  The little accordion on the rod looks like it's expanding completly.  I find that the rotation of that piece (I don't know how you call that) isn't much.  Normal?<P>The troubleshooting in my Haynes book tells me that:<BR>34 - Unable to select gears<BR>         1-Faulty transaxle<BR>         2-Faulty clutch disc<BR>         3-Fork and bearing not assembled properly.<BR>         4-Faulty pressure plate<BR>         5-Pressure plate-to-flywheel bolts loose.<P><BR>I have notice a slight "noise" from the rear when I press the clutch quickly and then releasing it.  If I go slowly, we don't hear it.  Upon release, it's like a little and pale "schkweek".<P>38 - Noise in clutch area<BR>         1-Fork shaft improperly installed<BR>         2-Faulty bearing<P>Coincidence? Do I have a prob with my pressure plate? I just find it so strange.  I was working ok just some little hours before.<P>I know, I write a lot.  I like to give a clear view of my analysis, and the details I have.  <P>I'm really sad I didn't drive it today.  I ride my friend's Talon just some minutes ago, but there's nothing like the Fiero feeling (maybe a good old Prelude '87 - but front-wheel-drive..  beurk!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Thanks to you who had the patience to read all that. May you have some ideas of my problem, some advises?  Technical tips?  Self experience? Thanks a lot.<P>BlackARROW, in a bit of a sorrow<BR>Owner of a standard black Fiero 2M4 '85||||reg||11
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||06-01-2001||11:12 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by BlackARROW:<P>Instead of pulling out the rod from the slave cylinder, I pushed it in.  It came back, but I think this is where everything started.<P>When this was done you emptied the slave cylinder of it's fluid and forced it back through the master cylinder.<P>I then did some things at the pedal, put some lube and the spring and in that plastic thing (with a "spring" in it - I guess. <P>?       What 'spring'?   Type of lube used? <P>I tried to bleed the clutch.  Actually I've done it some times.  Now I know how to proceed.  I used Archie's method (also, I bled the slave cylinder).  Since I lube some spring near the clutch pedal, I feel it less hard to action, but then it doesn't do any action still.  <P>All that should have been done at this point was to bleed the system through the slave. I'm not familar with the way Archie bleeds clutch hydrylics, Can you fill me in on his procedure?<P>Sounds as if you have all kinds of air in the system now. The slave cylinder's rod travel should be at least 1" (2.5mm) at the action arm of the throwout bearing fork rod.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||06-01-2001||11:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BlackARROW:<BR><B> (1)...One thing I had a hardtime (didn't suceed yet) is to remove the Thermostat cap  (you know, push and clockwise some bit - well it doesn't push very far.  Any tricks?)<P>(2)...I find that the rotation of that piece (I don't know how you call that) isn't much.  Normal?<P>(3)I like to give a clear view of my analysis, and the details I have.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Item (1): I ordinarily use an oil filter wrench, to get the thermostat cap off.<P>Item(2):  I have read that you want the slave cylinder's actuating rod to extend 15/16" (if I remember correctly), when you depress the clutch pedal.  Apparently, 7/8" won't do.  This is quoting Ed Parks' or Archies's website - I forget which.<P>Item(3):  Good thing.  I'm pretty wordy, too.  But getting a complete synopsis of the problems and how they arose sure beats, "My Fiero isn't working right. Any ideas?"   ||||reg||6
Z||000003||BlackARROW||06-02-2001||01:08 AM||||Mind if I call you Junior, Indiana? (Ref: Indiana Jones)<P>Well I don't know if you have that.  There's a plastic something attach to the clutch pedal.  I don't know what it's used for.  It was making some "squeek", "schlik" or "stzdadenspovitkz". I got some probs writing car sounds.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>The lube I used is General Purpose lube spray for gears and moving parts. (find it more efficient than the Graphite one, and it doesn't stink that long inside  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Archie's method in resume: no pumping (just a full push) for 5 bleeds + slave cylinder bleeding. <BR>Here's the link: [url]http://www.v8archie.com/arch4.htm<BR>[/url]<P>You think I have all kinds of air in the system? What should I do?  Restart with the bleeding?  How many shots?  Let's say I have air stuck inside, but the rod extends about an inch anyway - shouldn't it work?<P>Speaking of terms, when they're talking about the "fork", what is it exactly?  I'm just wondering, in case I'll have to re-install/re-assemble this (like they say in the Haynes' troubleshoot.<P>*sigh*<P>thanks<P>BA-&gt;<BR>I wanna ride||||reg||4
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||06-02-2001||08:58 AM||||Junior, . . . . . .HUMMMMmmmmm . . . . . no.<P>OK. The 'Plastic something' must be your safety switch which allows the car to start only if the clutch pedal is depressed. Typical little scrap/squeal when pressed.<P>I'm not sure about the lube your using, but as long as it is safe for plastic and rubber parts your OK.<P>Archies method will work well, but I've had some that have been very difficult to bleed. I've been using a vac. pump lately with better success and you can use it solo. I also jack you the left side rear of the car to raise the slave cylinder above the level of the master and the bleeder screw to be the highest point. Doing this any air in the system will naturally rise to the highest point, the bleeder screw. If you don't wish to purchase a vac pump, you can have an assistant top depress the pedal as Archie outlines. There will be no need to do the slave!<P>So, your rod moves about an inch (2.5mm) from the slave? Does it stay there with the pedal depressed, or will it move back while holding the pedal to the floor? If it moves back, you do have air someplace!<P>The 'Fork' in reference is the fork on the rod which moves the throw-out against the fingers of the pressure plate, which disengages the clutch disk to allow shifting. Requires R&R of the transmission.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jncomutt||06-02-2001||10:42 AM||||Not really too sure about all your details there making things complicated, but that sounds like the same problem I had.<P>I heard the noises, bled it over and over, still couldn't get much clutch action.  I found the problem to be so much simpler than I had thought, the clutch pedal was bent.  It didn't look it at all, compared to the other, it was almost un-noticable.  But it made a BIG difference.<P>You might want to check to see if the bracket that comes off of the clutch pedal (the part the master cylinder rod connects to) is slightly twisted.  If its even pulled up just a bit, you will be pushing the clutch to the floor, but not moving the rod down all the way.<P>Sorry if my description is kind of awkward, but I think you get the idea.  Just before you go nuts searching for the problem, try the pedal...||||reg||1
Z||000006||BlackARROW||06-02-2001||01:44 PM||||Indiana:  I don't think my "plastic something" works anyway, so you must be right.  For the rod, well it didn't move back.   I'll be thinking about that vac pump for futur bleeding. Thanks<P>Incomutt: Hmmm.. I shall try something with that bend pedal you're talking about.  If you tell me that even the sligh bend can affect that, and bracket too (twisted).  I got the idea.  My resume here is as awkward I guess.<BR>Thanks anyway, I went nuts already. Was thinking about the whole thing, master / slave cylinder, but they work fine..  Ahhhh... I'll check upon that, and give you a result.  <P>Thanks again||||reg||1
Z||000007||EdsB52||06-02-2001||02:53 PM||||Hey BlackARROW, mind if we call you junior in reference to your lack of Fiero experience? I didn't have 2 days to decipher the clutch problem, but in regard to the thermostat cap, I believe you turn it counterclockwise to remove it, unless you're in the Southern Hemisphere.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rodrv6||06-02-2001||03:33 PM||||If all else in the clutch system seems to be OK, you might want to look at the lever on the transaxle that the slave cylinder pushes against.  Some cars had a stamped steel lever that is prone to cracking.  If it cracks it will become somewhat flexible and cause the clutch not to completely disengage.  GM redesigned the lever in later cars to a solid forged piece.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000009||BlackARROW||06-02-2001||06:00 PM||||EdsB52 : you may call me Junior.<P>First, I'm not a mechanic but a driver who likes to race. I like to learn though how all this is working together. I just like to do the repairs myself. Still, I have no license to practice mechanic wherever, and probably will never have.  Deusio, the thermostat cap I can't turn it at all, I can't even push it. It says in the book "push down firmly and twist it clockwise".  You know, I was just asking for a trick. No need to show off.<P>I didn't have 2 days to decipher you're special.  hehe||||reg||1
Z||000010||BlackARROW||06-02-2001||06:03 PM||||Rodrv6:  I'll look at that lever. I was hoping it was not related up to there, but if you're right, well be it.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>thanks<P>(330,000 miles? Impressive)||||reg||1
Z||000011||BlackARROW||06-03-2001||09:58 PM||||I just worked on it again, or rather analysing what's going on.  <P>The noise has come more evident from the rear:<P>as I press on the clutch pedal, from 1/4 to 7/8 pedal press, I hear something like a spring compressing. <P>Is that normal to hear something like that from the clutch?  I guess not. I wonder what it could be; I don't really know how a clutch really works. I've got a graph, but that's it. My concern is: can I do it myself now? My Haynes book just tell me: "38. Noise in clutch area - Fork shaft improperly installed or Faulty bearing." What noise anyway? I really got to find a solution here, as my BlackARROW is parked in a sub-basement parking lot.. 3rd stage :/  How could this happen suddently. Coincidence, as I was refering at first?<P>Who's familiar with the inside mechanism of the clutch?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>merci||||reg||1
Z||000012||PAUL88GT||06-04-2001||12:31 AM||||I bet the original aluminum clutch pedal is bent and you are not getting the right amount of throw.  I changed the one on my 86se and it completely solved my disengagement problem. The new lever is about $30 from gmpartsdirect.com. Perform a search in the techical section. It not too hard.  Archie has an excellent procedure.||||reg||1
Z||000013||BlackARROW||06-04-2001||09:01 PM||||I've search and found something interesting too.  On the "Fiero Online Service Guide", it is said that there is a problem with the Clutch Fork bushing, that this thing is too tight.  Here's the site for anyone who would have the same thing: <A HREF="http://thefieroclub.com/osg/fiero_osg/clutch-bushings.html" TARGET=_blank>http://thefieroclub.com/osg/fiero_osg/clutch-bushings.html</A> <P><BR>Good news in a way, I can't get into any gears if I start on neutral, then try to get in.  But if I start in first gear (while trying to old the clutch), well I can get it going in all the gears, with some tightness though. Needless to say that I'm pushing the pedal to the floor, and that it grips after only an inch on the release. It's just one symptom of the bushing problem.  Eh.<P>So I'm thinking to fix both things. Bushing, the pedal and maybe the fork itself.<P>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000014||Jncomutt||06-04-2001||09:09 PM||||In reply to RodRV6, I just fell victim to that clutch arm this weekend.  My 84 SE suddenly wouldn't go from 2nd to 3rd while I was driving.  I pulled over, opened up the back, and sure enough, it was split across where the hole is drilled through (or stamped, how ever it got there)  To make matters worse, The Fiero Store doesn't carry this part, as it has been discontinued, and all the yards 'round here only have 86 & 87s.<P>I made a temporary solution for now.  I took the arm off of my 86 parts car, and attached that one.  I then moved the rubber stop out of its seat, and wedged one in above it, so 2 of them sit in that space.  I guided the slave cyl. rod into the lower one and it lined up.  Not very trusty, for that top rubber could come loose, but its 150 miles and is still doin well til I find that replacement.||||reg||1
